1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,750
Holly: Hello Dr Green - I'm here to talk to you about my work placement.

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,110
Tutor: Oh yes, it's Holly, isn't it?

3
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:08,020
Holly: Yes.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:10,650
Tutor: So, which work placement have you chosen?

5
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:14,103
Holly: I decided to go for the Orion Stadium placement.

6
00:00:14,103 --> 00:00:17,460
The event I'll be managing is one

7
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:21,700
where I'm helping to set up a sports competition for primary school children.

8
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:24,160
Tutor: Yup. That's always a popular placement -

9
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,850
even though it can be tougher than you think working with children.

10
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:31,939
Holly: I know, but it's the fresh air that attracts me -

11
00:00:31,939 --> 00:00:35,100
organising something indoors doesn't have the same appeal

12
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:37,050
even though it might be fun.

13
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:42,660
Tutor: OK, so obviously safety's going to be one of your key concerns for this event.

14
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:45,720
Holly: Yes, I've already thought about that

15
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:49,530
I'll need to make sure none of the equipment's damaged.

16
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:51,980
Tutor: Ah well, you'll be working with schools

17
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:55,337
so the equipment will be their responsibility.

18
00:00:55,337 --> 00:00:59,330
However, the grounds and what goes on there will be yours.

19
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:06,810
Holly: Oh I see - that'll include keeping everyone within the boundary once they're in their kit and on the field?

20
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:13,643
Tutor: Exactly - you'll need to inspect areas like changing rooms as well for anything someone can trip over,

21
00:01:13,643 --> 00:01:17,240
but your main priority will be not to lose anyone!

22
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:21,300
Holly: Right. I'll need staff to help with that.

23
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:23,860
Tutor: And don't forget about the spectators.

24
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:27,280
Holly: Mmm. I was thinking that many of them will be parents

25
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,300
who could help run the event.

26
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,280
Tutor: I wouldn't rely on that

27
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:34,860
They'll be more interested in filming their children than volunteering.

28
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:38,730
Holly: I'll need to make sure they don't interfere with events doing that!

29
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:40,920
Tutor: And that's not always easy,

30
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,920
especially when a proud parent's trying to get a snap of their child

31
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:47,490
and you want them to move elsewhere.

32
00:01:47,490 --> 00:01:51,390
Holly: OK. What about the scheduling?

33
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:56,707
Tutor: With sporting events there are all sorts of things that can alter the timetable -

34
00:01:56,707 --> 00:02:01,670
like rain, for instance - though so far, we've always been lucky with that.

35
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:07,184
Holly: Yeah, and I was thinking about what to do if someone got hurt as well.

36
00:02:07,184 --> 00:02:10,630
I know that last year that caused a terrible delay.

37
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:13,360
Tutor: You have to be prepared for such things.

38
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,100
Holly: Oh. What if a match ends in a draw

39
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:20,510
do you let the teams keep going until someone wins?

40
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:24,610
Tutor: That'll be up to you - and again, you need to plan for it.

41
00:02:24,610 --> 00:02:27,000
Holly: Right.

42
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,738
Tutor: Now, the aim of your work placement is to give you the opportunity to develop the skills that an events manager needs.

43
00:02:34,738 --> 00:02:37,430
So, let's talk about those a bit.

44
00:02:37,430 --> 00:02:40,915
Holly: Well, I think my communication skills are pretty good.

45
00:02:40,915 --> 00:02:46,490
I can talk on the phone to people and book venues and that kind of thing.

46
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:50,553
Tutor: Good - just remember it isn't only about what you say.

47
00:02:50,553 --> 00:02:55,970
If you meet someone face-to-face and want to persuade them to be a sponsor, for example...

48
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:58,610
Holly: Oh, I'll dress up for that! Sure.

49
00:02:58,610 --> 00:03:03,050
Tutor: Good. Let's go on to think about your organisational skills.

50
00:03:03,050 --> 00:03:06,420
You're working in a very people-based industry

51
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:09,710
and that means things won't always go to plan.

52
00:03:09,710 --> 00:03:13,540
Holly: I guess it's being prepared to make changes that matters.

53
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:18,086
Tutor: That's right. You may have to make an on. The-spot change to a timetable

54
00:03:18,086 --> 00:03:20,510
because of a problem you hadn't anticipated...

55
00:03:20,510 --> 00:03:23,000
Holly:... just do it! OK.

56
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,690
Tutor: How's your time management these days?

57
00:03:25,690 --> 00:03:30,000
Holly: I'm working on it - I'm certainly better when I have a deadline

58
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,160
which is why this work suits me.

59
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:36,140
Tutor: Yes, but it's how you respond as that deadline approaches!

60
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,130
Holly: I know I've got to look calm even if I'm in a panic.

61
00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:44,070
Tutor: Just think to yourself - no one must know I'm under pressure.

62
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:48,030
Holly: Yeah - even though I'm multi-tasking like crazy!

63
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:51,040
Tutor: Another skill that events managers need is creativity.

64
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,718
Often your client has what we call the 'big picture' idea,

65
00:03:54,718 --> 00:04:01,740
but it's up to the events manager to think of all the fine points that go to making it work.

66
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:05,660
Holly: Right, so I need to listen carefully to that idea

67
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:08,000
and then fill in all the gaps.

68
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,780
Tutor: That's right. And you'll have a team working under you

69
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,279
so another key skill is leadership.

70
00:04:15,279 --> 00:04:21,792
Your team may have lots of ideas too, but you've got to make the ultimate choices.

71
00:04:21,792 --> 00:04:25,240
Do we have refreshments inside or out, for example?

72
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:28,070
Holly: Isn't it better to be democratic?

73
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:32,417
Tutor: It's a nice idea, but you have the ultimate responsibility.

74
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:34,953
So, believe in what you think best.

75
00:04:34,953 --> 00:04:40,750
Be prepared to say 'yes', that's a good idea but it won't work here.

76
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:42,792
Holly: I see what you mean.

77
00:04:42,792 --> 00:04:45,174
What about the networking side of things?

78
00:04:45,174 --> 00:04:49,420
I know it's an area that a lot of students worry about

79
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:53,000
because we don't have much experience to offer others.

80
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Tutor: But even without it - you can still be an interesting person with useful ideas.

81
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,200
And the more people you impress, the better.

82
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:05,620
Holly: I guess that will help me when I apply for a real job.

83
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:10,180
Tutor: Exactly - think ahead - remember what your ambitions are

84
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:11,530
and keep them in mind.

85
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:13,000
Holly: Definitely.
